latest_plan = "Based on the reflection and insights gained, here's a detailed step-by-step plan to solve the challenge:\n\n1. Analyze the input grid:\n   a. Determine the dimensions of the input grid.\n   b. Identify the core pattern by finding the smallest rectangle that contains all non-zero cells.\n   c. Check if the pattern touches any edges of the input grid.\n\n2. Determine the output grid size:\n   a. Calculate the size as (2n-1) x (2n-1), where n is the maximum dimension of the input grid.\n\n3. Extract and prepare the core pattern:\n   a. Copy the core pattern identified in step 1b.\n   b. If necessary, scale up the core pattern slightly to ensure it will be visible in the output.\n\n4. Create the quadrupled pattern:\n   a. Place the core pattern in the top-left quadrant of the output grid.\n   b. Rotate the core pattern 90 degrees and place it in the top-right quadrant.\n   c. Rotate again and place in the bottom-right quadrant.\n   d. Rotate once more and place in the bottom-left quadrant.\n\n5. Enhance symmetry and complexity:\n   a. Identify any gaps or inconsistencies in the quadrupled pattern.\n   b. Fill these gaps in a way that enhances the overall symmetry and complexity.\n   c. Ensure that the pattern flows smoothly between the quadrants.\n\n6. Create central void:\n   a. Clear a small area (e.g., 3x3 or 5x5) in the very center of the output grid.\n\n7. Extend to edges if necessary:\n   a. If the input pattern touched any edge, extend the output pattern to touch all edges.\n   b. This may involve mirroring edge cells to the opposite edge.\n\n8. Refine the pattern:\n   a. Look for isolated cells (both colored and black) that break the pattern's flow.\n   b. Adjust these cells to enhance the overall symmetry and consistency of the pattern.\n\n9. Ensure corner cells are black:\n   a. Set all four corner cells of the output grid to 0 (black).\n\n10. Final adjustments:\n    a. Review the entire output grid for any remaining inconsistencies or asymmetries.\n    b. Make minor adjustments as needed to perfect the pattern.\n\n11. Create and return the final ColoredGrid:\n    a. Construct a new ColoredGrid object with the transformed pattern.\n    b. Ensure this object is always returned, never None.\n\n12. Validation:\n    a. Verify that the output has perfect rotational symmetry.\n    b. Confirm that it touches all edges if the input touched any edge.\n    c. Validate that the corners are black.\n    d. Ensure the overall pattern maintains the essence of the input while being more complex.\n\nThis plan focuses on capturing the essence of the input pattern and transforming it into a more complex, symmetrical output. It allows for flexibility in handling different input patterns while ensuring consistent, visually appealing results that meet all the challenge requirements."
